    <solution><alert> Solution:</alert> 



a) Write out the terms for each value of <m>i</m> from 2 to 4,
then add the terms.
<me>\begin{array}{rcl}
    i = 2 \amp \to\amp  a_{2} = (2)^2 = 4\\
    i = 3 \amp \to\amp  a_{3} = (3)^2 = 9\\
    i = 4 \amp \to\amp  a_{4} = (4)^2 = 16\\
  \end{array}</me>
The sum is: <m>\displaystyle\sum_{i=2}^{4} i^2  = 29</m> 




b) Write out the terms for each value of <m>i</m> from -1 to 4,
then add the terms.
<me>\begin{array}{rcl}
    i = -1 \amp \to\amp  a_{-1} = 2(-1)  - 5 = -7\\
    i = 0 \amp \to\amp  a_{0} = 2(0)  - 5 = -5\\
    i = 1 \amp \to\amp  a_{1} = 2(1)  - 5 = -3\\
    i = 2 \amp \to\amp  a_{2} = 2(2)  - 5 = -1\\
    i = 3 \amp \to\amp  a_{3} = 2(3)  - 5 = 1\\
    i = 4 \amp \to\amp  a_{4} = 2(4)  - 5 = 3\\
  \end{array}</me>
The sum is: <m>\displaystyle\sum_{i=-1}^{4} (2 i  - 5)  = -12</m> 


</solution>
